Time complexity: `O(n log D)`, where `D` is the range between the smallest and largest bloom days.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This keeps a current window length of adjacent flowers that are ready by `day`. Every time the window reaches `k`, it is consumed as one bouquet and starts at zero. That reset matters because a flower cannot be reused in two bouquets.

There are not enough flowers when `m * k > n`, including cases where the multiplication itself could overflow a normal integer. In that case the answer is immediately `-1`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int roseGarden(int n, vector<int> nums, int k, int m) {
        if (1LL * m * k > n) {
            return -1;
        }

        int low = 1;
        int high = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int answer = -1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int day = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (possible(nums, k, m, day)) {
                answer = day;
                high = day - 1;
            } else {
                low = day + 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

private:
    bool possible(const vector<int>& nums, int k, int needed, int day) {
        int readyWindow = 0;
        int bouquets = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            if (nums[i] > day) {
                readyWindow = 0;
                continue;
            }

            ++readyWindow;
            if (readyWindow == k) {
                ++bouquets;
                readyWindow = 0;
            }

            if (bouquets == needed) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log M)`, where `M` is the largest bloom day.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the copied `nums` parameter.

An empty input is not expected by the platform because `m` and `k` require flowers, but the flower-count check handles the normal impossible case before the search.

The array does not need to be changed. For a test day, every continuous block of flowers with `nums[i] <= day` can make `blockSize / k` bouquets. This avoids forming bouquets one by one and makes the feasibility check easy to read.

The search uses the smallest and largest bloom days as its limits. For example, with `[1, 10, 3, 10, 2]`, `m = 3`, and `k = 1`, day `3` gives three separate usable blocks and is valid.

